      resumen: "An association filed a complaint against comedian Héctor de Miguel (artistically \"Quequé\") for alleged expressions of hatred poured out in his program Hora Veintipico. The Madrid Investigating Court nº 38 processed the comedian for a alleged crime of hatred and against religious feelings. Both the defense and the Public Prosecutor's Office appealed the decision and, through Ruling nº 379/2025 of April 30, 2025, Madrid Provincial Court Section 30 upheld both appeals and ordered the dismissal of proceedings, considering that the statements were protected by freedom of expression and artistic creation.",
      hechos: "On June 27, 2024, during the radio and comedy program Hora Veintipico on Cadena SER, Héctor de Miguel joked about \"filling the Valley of the Fallen cross with dynamite\" and throwing the resulting stones at pedophile priests. An association filed a complaint and the Investigating Court nº 38 admitted the accusation and agreed to continue through the summary procedure for a possible crime of hatred. The prosecution maintained that the expressions attributable to the defendant were not mere hyperbole but incited hostility against the Catholic religion. In response to this position, the defense argued that it was a satirical exercise protected by article 20 of the Constitution. After the proceedings were initiated, both the Public Prosecutor's Office and Héctor de Miguel's representation filed an appeal against the order that had processed him. The Madrid Provincial Court, Section 30, examined the context and humorous tone of the statements and concluded that there was no incitement to hatred or real hostility. Therefore, it revoked the investigating court's decision and ordered the dismissal of proceedings.",
      estrategia: "The strategy focused on highlighting the satirical and artistic character of the denounced expressions, invoking constitutional protection of freedom of expression and creation (art. 20 of the Constitution). The defense argued that political humor, even when provocative or offensive, is part of public debate and cannot be criminally punished without affecting pluralism. The expansive interpretation of the hatred crime (art. 510 CP) was challenged and the limits required by case law were recalled: there must be real and serious incitement to hostility or violence against a group, a circumstance that did not exist in the case. In addition, the complainant's lack of standing to defend third-party religious feelings was reproached and the prosecution's support was counted on.",
      pruebas: "The evidence was limited to the radio program recording where the phrases were uttered, as well as the transcripts provided by the complaint. There were no witnesses attesting to any hostile climate or acts of violence or discrimination afterwards. In the appeal, the absence of concrete damage or risk was highlighted and the lack of follow-up of the words in the following days. The defense provided constitutional case law and precedents in which complaints based on humorous speeches were archived. Finally, the appealed order, together with the Public Prosecutor's appeal and the defense's appeal, were assessed by the Provincial Court.",
      resolucion: "The Madrid Provincial Court (Section 30) issued Ruling nº 379/2025 of April 30, 2025 in which it upheld the appeals filed by the Public Prosecutor's Office and Héctor de Miguel's representation. It revoked the Investigating Court nº 38's order of January 23, 2025 and, pursuant to article 637.1 of the Criminal Procedure Law, decreed the dismissal of proceedings. The Chamber understood that satirical statements do not constitute a crime of hatred nor do they promote hostility against Catholics. This ruling was not subject to appeal. The result was fully favorable to the client, who was released from any imputation.",
      marco_legal: "The case revolved around the balance between freedom of expression and artistic creation (art. 20 CE) and the limits of the hatred crime typified in article 510 of the Criminal Code. The Provincial Court underlined that, to appreciate the hatred crime, real and direct incitement to violence or discrimination against a group is required. In the absence of this requirement and given the humorous context, dismissal of proceedings is appropriate pursuant to article 637.1 LECrim. The ruling also reproduces constitutional doctrine protecting satirical expressions that, although they may seem offensive, form part of democratic debate and do not exceed criminal law limits."
